WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.593
Hi, I'm Scarf!

00:00:02.593 --> 00:00:06.417
A post says a merged kernel change leaks a dense Qwen four model.

00:00:06.417 --> 00:00:08.476
Let's see what the code really shows.

00:00:10.308 --> 00:00:13.978
The change is real, but it says far less than the post claims.

00:00:13.978 --> 00:00:16.518
And the architecture it points at is already public.

00:00:19.883 --> 00:00:23.543
The screenshot names two per-rank shapes: Q twelve over K V two,

00:00:23.543 --> 00:00:26.898
and a hidden width of five thousand one hundred and twenty.

00:00:27.318 --> 00:00:32.899
Both match the released twenty-seven-billion model: twenty-four query heads over four K V heads.

00:00:33.319 --> 00:00:36.475
What the dense model adds is already public in vLLM.

00:00:37.958 --> 00:00:42.601
A token climbs three Gated DeltaNet layers, then one sparse attention layer,

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:44.922
repeated, with a dense feed-forward block.

00:00:45.342 --> 00:00:47.500
Beside every layer run residual lanes.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.816
Four is Flash-Next's count, so for the dense model it is reasoned.

00:00:52.236 --> 00:00:56.226
The sparse layer compresses keys into blocks of four, and reads only

00:00:56.226 --> 00:00:57.888
two thousand and forty-eight tokens.

00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:02.143
Dense attention reads every earlier key.

00:01:02.143 --> 00:01:07.146
At two hundred and sixty-two thousand tokens, that is seventeen gigabytes a step.

00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:11.548
Sparse attention first compresses keys into blocks of four, and scores them.

00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:13.538
It keeps the best blocks.

00:01:13.538 --> 00:01:15.737
The read stays near two thousand tokens.

00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:19.340
Decoding one stream is bound by memory.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.771
Bandwidth is the card's memory speed.

00:01:23.001 --> 00:01:24.668
Each step streams the weights once.

00:01:26.508 --> 00:01:30.791
Reasoned ceilings: about thirty tokens a second on one card at INT4,

00:01:30.791 --> 00:01:32.218
and sixty on two.

00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:35.599
A kernel change proves kernels exist.

00:01:35.599 --> 00:01:38.849
The shapes are inference, and the release is unconfirmed.

00:01:40.983 --> 00:01:44.435
To recap: a real change, shapes carried over, and no release.

00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:47.605
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:47.605 --> 00:01:48.183
I'm Scarf.

00:01:48.183 --> 00:01:48.472
Bye!
